WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Let's spend a few minutes going over the mechanics of sub-agent delegation, writing

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.579
a precise prompt, defining a return contract, scoping the tool palette, and recognizing

3
00:00:11.579 --> 00:00:14.939
the failure modes that undermine the whole pattern.

4
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:19.420
Getting delegation right in practice means knowing all four of these pieces, not just

5
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:20.659
the high-level idea.

6
00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:23.860
Let's work through each one.

7
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.159
Delegation succeeds or fails on the shape of the handoff.

8
00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:32.880
The return contract is the explicit shape of the response the sub-agent produces, defined

9
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.900
up front so the parent's context stays clean after the sub-agent returns.

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.700
The trust boundary is the architectural rule that the parent treats sub-agent output as

11
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.939
a hypothesis, to verify, not a definitive truth.

12
00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:52.819
And sub-agent delegation is the pattern itself, spawning a child agent to perform verbose

13
00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:56.860
investigation and returning only a focused summary.

14
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.979
The mechanics in this lesson all exist to make those ideas work reliably in a real system,

15
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.540
not just as an abstract design principle.

16
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.739
Start with the prompt.

17
00:01:07.739 --> 00:01:12.919
A sub-agent prompt is a precise question, not an open-ended task, and a well-formed

18
00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:15.339
one specifies four things.

19
00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:20.959
The question, stated as a single concrete ask, rather than a broad directive.

20
00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:26.540
The scope, naming which directories or file types to search and which to ignore.

21
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.919
The return shape, describing exactly what format the answer should take.

22
00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:37.720
And the stop condition, telling the sub-agent when to return what it has, rather than continuing to search.

23
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.160
Without a stop condition, a sub-agent can spin on a hard lookup and return late with

24
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.120
more information than the parent needs.

25
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.220
Vague prompts produce vague answers that re-pollute the parent's context, so the quality of the

26
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.319
outcome tracks the quality of the prompt you write.

27
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.220
Compare a vague prompt to a precise one directly.

28
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.739
A vague prompt says, find the authentication logic and explain how it works.

29
00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:07.459
The sub-agent reads broadly, follows tangents, and comes back with a narrative.

30
00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:10.179
The parent receives a wall of text.

31
00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:11.339
Context is polluted.

32
00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:17.979
A precise prompt says, return the file path and function name that handles JWT validation

33
00:02:17.979 --> 00:02:23.899
in one file path and one function name, with one sentence of context, nothing else.

34
00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:26.160
The parent gets exactly what it needs.

35
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.759
The difference between those two prompts is the entire difference between delegation working

36
00:02:31.759 --> 00:02:35.440
and delegation failing to deliver value.

37
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.080
The return contract is the specification for what the sub-agent hands back.

38
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.440
A structured finding beats a narrative every time.

39
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.960
A strong default shape is, file path, line number, brief summary.

40
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:53.279
That gives the parent enough to act on without loading it with content it didn't ask for.

41
00:02:53.279 --> 00:02:57.940
You should also specify a maximum length explicitly in the prompt.

42
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.380
Without a length cap, a sub-agent that found something interesting has no guidance about

43
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.820
how much to return.

44
00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.979
A well-defined contract keeps parent context clean, regardless of how much the sub-agent

45
00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:11.660
read during its investigation.

46
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.779
The contract is the guardrail.

47
00:03:14.779 --> 00:03:18.619
Scoping the sub-agent's tool palette is the third mechanical decision.

48
00:03:18.619 --> 00:03:22.580
Give the sub-agent enough tools to investigate, and no more than that.

49
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.660
Read tools and search tools belong on the palette.

50
00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.660
Write tools, delete tools, and API call tools generally don't.

51
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.460
The sub-agent's job is to find things, not to change them.

52
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:39.779
A narrow palette prevents accidental side effects during discovery, and keeps the sub-agent's

53
00:03:39.779 --> 00:03:42.619
behavior predictable and auditable.

54
00:03:42.619 --> 00:03:48.259
If you let a discovery sub-agent write files or make API calls, you have turned a low-risk

55
00:03:48.259 --> 00:03:51.960
investigation into something with real consequences.

56
00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.479
Keep the palette small and read-only as the default.

57
00:03:56.479 --> 00:04:02.639
The trust boundary is the rule that the parent treats sub-agent output as a hypothesis, not

58
00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:05.080
a verified truth.

59
00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.800
Sub-agents can be wrong.

60
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.919
They can misread a function, miss a file that overrides the one they found, or return a

61
00:04:11.919 --> 00:04:14.720
stale result from a cached document.

62
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.299
For low-stakes lookups, proceeding directly on the sub-agent's answer is usually fine.

63
00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:25.540
For critical path decisions, like identifying the function you are about to modify, or the

64
00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.820
config you are about to update, the parent should spot-check the finding before acting.

65
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:35.980
Calibrating how much trust to extend based on what is at stake is the practical skill

66
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.380
the boundary concept is really about.

67
00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:42.940
Three failure modes show up repeatedly in practice.

68
00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.739
The first is a wall-of-text return, where the sub-agent ignores the return contract

69
00:04:47.739 --> 00:04:49.619
and sends back a narrative.

70
00:04:49.619 --> 00:04:53.700
This re-pollutes the parent's context and defeats the whole purpose.

71
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.700
The second is scope creep, where the sub-agent reads beyond the specified scope and returns

72
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.179
extra findings the parent didn't request, adding noise and latency to the interaction.

73
00:05:04.179 --> 00:05:09.220
The third is blind trust, where the parent acts on sub-agent output, without verifying

74
00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.779
critical findings, and propagates errors into downstream work.

75
00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:18.420
All three failures trace back to the same root cause, insufficient specification in

76
00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.540
the original prompt.

77
00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:25.179
Tight prompts and clean returns is the idea to carry forward here.

78
00:05:25.179 --> 00:05:30.019
The return contract carries most of the weight, because an uncapped answer walks the same

79
00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:34.619
verbosity right back to the context you delegated to protect.

80
00:05:34.619 --> 00:05:40.500
A sub-agent isn't a smaller you, it's a narrow question with a shape on the answer,

81
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.700
and whatever comes back is still a claim worth checking.

82
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.260
Keep that in mind.

83
00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:46.600
That wraps up this lesson.

84
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.739
I'll see you in the next one.

